1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,660
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science A,

2
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:11,614
Unit 1, part two: topics one point six to
one point ten. We'll look at shortcuts,

3
00:00:11,614 --> 00:00:15,509
libraries, documentation, and how methods
are called.

4
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:22,249
Programmers update variables all the time.
Add points to a score. Count one more

5
00:00:22,249 --> 00:00:22,806
visitor.

6
00:00:23,256 --> 00:00:27,629
Java has shortcuts for this, called
compound assignment operators.

7
00:00:28,779 --> 00:00:34,240
Instead of writing x equals x plus five,
you can write x plus equals five.

8
00:00:34,690 --> 00:00:36,525
They do exactly the same thing.

9
00:00:36,975 --> 00:00:42,317
The same shortcut works for every
arithmetic operator: minus equals, times

10
00:00:42,317 --> 00:00:45,060
equals, divide equals, and mod equals.

11
00:00:46,210 --> 00:00:50,818
Adding or subtracting one is so common
that it has its own operators.

12
00:00:51,268 --> 00:00:56,047
Count plus plus adds one to count. Count
minus minus subtracts one.

13
00:00:56,497 --> 00:01:02,014
On the AP exam, you'll see these used as
their own statements, most often inside

14
00:01:02,014 --> 00:01:02,428
loops.

15
00:01:03,578 --> 00:01:05,135
Let's trace a few lines.

16
00:01:05,585 --> 00:01:09,297
n starts at ten. n plus equals three makes
it thirteen.

17
00:01:09,747 --> 00:01:15,614
n times equals two makes it twenty six. n
minus minus makes it twenty five.

18
00:01:16,064 --> 00:01:22,044
And n mod equals seven gives the remainder
of twenty five divided by seven, which is

19
00:01:22,044 --> 00:01:22,400
four.

20
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:28,350
Careful with divide equals on an int. It
still uses integer division.

21
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:34,474
If k is seven, then k divide equals two
makes k three, not three point five.

22
00:01:35,624 --> 00:01:41,219
Your turn. a starts at five. a times
equals three. a minus equals four. a plus

23
00:01:41,219 --> 00:01:43,155
plus. What is a at the end?

24
00:01:43,605 --> 00:01:44,885
Pause the video now.

25
00:01:45,335 --> 00:01:49,943
Five times three is fifteen. Minus four is
eleven. Plus one is twelve.

26
00:01:50,393 --> 00:01:53,358
Next, we'll start using APIs and
libraries.

27
00:01:54,508 --> 00:01:57,367
Good programmers don't write everything
from scratch.

28
00:01:57,817 --> 00:02:03,080
Java comes with thousands of ready made
classes you can use. In this lesson,

29
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:07,374
you'll learn what libraries and APIs are,
and how to use them.

30
00:02:08,524 --> 00:02:13,153
A library is a collection of classes that
someone already wrote and tested.

31
00:02:13,603 --> 00:02:18,756
An API, or application programming
interface, describes how to use them:

32
00:02:18,756 --> 00:02:24,196
which classes exist, what methods they
have, and what those methods need and

33
00:02:24,196 --> 00:02:24,697
return.

34
00:02:25,147 --> 00:02:29,861
You don't need to know how a method works
inside. You just need its API.

35
00:02:31,011 --> 00:02:35,001
Java's standard library includes classes
you'll use all year.

36
00:02:35,451 --> 00:02:40,653
String for text. Math for calculations.
Scanner for input. And ArrayList for

37
00:02:40,653 --> 00:02:43,323
lists, which comes later in the course.

38
00:02:44,473 --> 00:02:48,355
A class is like a blueprint. Objects are
the things built from it.

39
00:02:48,805 --> 00:02:54,100
A class defines attributes, which are the
data each object holds, and behaviors,

40
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,549
which are the methods it can perform.

41
00:02:56,999 --> 00:03:01,757
For example, a String object holds
characters, and has methods like length.

42
00:03:02,907 --> 00:03:05,680
Java organizes classes into packages.

43
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:11,921
To use a class from another package,
import it at the top of your file. Scanner

44
00:03:11,921 --> 00:03:17,053
lives in java dot util, so you write
import java dot util dot Scanner.

45
00:03:17,503 --> 00:03:22,601
Classes in java dot lang, like String and
Math, are imported automatically.

46
00:03:23,751 --> 00:03:29,286
Here's a line from the Math class's API:
public static double square root, which

47
00:03:29,286 --> 00:03:30,877
takes a double named a.

48
00:03:31,327 --> 00:03:36,082
It tells you the method's name, square
root. It takes one double. And it returns

49
00:03:36,082 --> 00:03:36,617
a double.

50
00:03:37,067 --> 00:03:42,170
That's everything you need to call it:
Math dot square root of twenty five gives

51
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:43,190
five point zero.

52
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:49,345
Your turn. Which of these classes needs an
import statement: String, Math, or

53
00:03:49,345 --> 00:03:49,865
Scanner?

54
00:03:50,315 --> 00:03:51,595
Pause the video now.

55
00:03:52,045 --> 00:03:57,377
Only Scanner. It's in java dot util.
String and Math are in java dot lang,

56
00:03:57,377 --> 00:03:59,683
which is imported automatically.

57
00:04:00,133 --> 00:04:02,949
Next, we'll document our own code with
comments.

58
00:04:04,099 --> 00:04:06,659
Code is read far more often than it's
written.

59
00:04:07,109 --> 00:04:11,568
Comments explain your code to other
people, and to you, a month from now.

60
00:04:12,018 --> 00:04:17,213
In this lesson, you'll learn Java's three
kinds of comments, and what preconditions

61
00:04:17,213 --> 00:04:18,652
and postconditions are.

62
00:04:19,802 --> 00:04:24,048
Comments are notes in your code that the
compiler completely ignores.

63
00:04:24,498 --> 00:04:29,799
Two slashes start a single line comment.
Everything after them on that line is

64
00:04:29,799 --> 00:04:30,343
ignored.

65
00:04:30,793 --> 00:04:36,596
Slash star starts a block comment that can
span many lines. It ends with star slash.

66
00:04:37,746 --> 00:04:42,567
The third kind is a Javadoc comment. It
starts with slash star star.

67
00:04:43,017 --> 00:04:48,030
Javadoc comments go right above a class or
method, and describe what it does.

68
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:54,057
Tags like at param describe each
parameter, and at return describes what

69
00:04:54,057 --> 00:05:00,022
the method returns. Tools can turn these
into web pages of API documentation.

70
00:05:01,172 --> 00:05:03,454
Methods often come with a precondition.

71
00:05:03,904 --> 00:05:09,426
A precondition is something that must be
true before the method is called. For

72
00:05:09,426 --> 00:05:11,904
example, the divisor b is not zero.

73
00:05:12,354 --> 00:05:16,344
The method doesn't check it. It's up to
the caller to make sure it's true.

74
00:05:17,494 --> 00:05:22,902
A postcondition is something that will be
true after the method finishes, as long as

75
00:05:22,902 --> 00:05:24,640
the preconditions were met.

76
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:28,397
For example, the method returns a divided
by b.

77
00:05:28,847 --> 00:05:34,500
Together, preconditions and postconditions
describe exactly what a method promises.

78
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:41,304
Your turn. Which comment style is used to
write Javadoc documentation for a method?

79
00:05:41,754 --> 00:05:43,034
Pause the video now.

80
00:05:43,484 --> 00:05:46,961
Slash star star. That's what marks a
Javadoc comment.

81
00:05:47,411 --> 00:05:49,459
Next, we'll look at method signatures.

82
00:05:50,609 --> 00:05:53,617
Methods are the building blocks of Java
programs.

83
00:05:54,067 --> 00:05:59,155
Before you can call a method, you need to
read its signature. This lesson shows you

84
00:05:59,155 --> 00:05:59,400
how.

85
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:03,857
A method is a named block of code that
does one task.

86
00:06:04,307 --> 00:06:09,320
Here's a method header: public static int
max, with two int parameters, a and b.

87
00:06:09,770 --> 00:06:14,784
The method's signature is its name plus
its parameter list: max, int, int.

88
00:06:15,234 --> 00:06:20,141
The int before the name is the return
type. It says this method gives back an

89
00:06:20,141 --> 00:06:20,396
int.

90
00:06:21,546 --> 00:06:24,405
Parameters and arguments are easy to mix
up.

91
00:06:24,855 --> 00:06:29,164
Parameters are the variables listed in the
method header, like a and b.

92
00:06:29,614 --> 00:06:35,026
Arguments are the actual values you pass
when you call it, like three and seven.

93
00:06:35,026 --> 00:06:37,934
Each argument is copied into its
parameter.

94
00:06:39,084 --> 00:06:43,116
Some methods don't give anything back.
Their return type is void.

95
00:06:43,566 --> 00:06:48,017
A void method does an action, like
printing, and you call it as its own

96
00:06:48,017 --> 00:06:48,644
statement.

97
00:06:49,094 --> 00:06:54,691
A non void method returns a value, which
you can store in a variable or use in an

98
00:06:54,691 --> 00:06:55,451
expression.

99
00:06:56,601 --> 00:07:01,231
Two methods can share a name if their
parameter lists are different. That's

100
00:07:01,231 --> 00:07:02,404
called overloading.

101
00:07:02,854 --> 00:07:07,355
For example, Math has an abs method for an
int, and another for a double.

102
00:07:07,805 --> 00:07:11,496
Java picks the right one by looking at the
arguments you pass.

103
00:07:12,646 --> 00:07:17,703
When you call a method, the arguments must
match the parameters in number, order, and

104
00:07:17,703 --> 00:07:17,000
type.

105
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:20,349
max of three and seven is fine.

106
00:07:20,799 --> 00:07:26,532
But max of three is an error, because it
needs two arguments. And max of three and

107
00:07:26,532 --> 00:07:31,146
four point five is an error, because four
point five isn't an int.

108
00:07:32,296 --> 00:07:37,570
Your turn. Here's a header: public static
double area, with a double named radius.

109
00:07:37,570 --> 00:07:40,850
What's the signature, and what type does
it return?

110
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,580
Pause the video now.

111
00:07:43,030 --> 00:07:46,166
The signature is area, double. It returns
a double.

112
00:07:46,616 --> 00:07:49,688
Next, we'll call class methods and trace
what happens.

113
00:07:50,838 --> 00:07:56,428
You know how to read a method's signature.
Now let's call methods, and follow exactly

114
00:07:56,428 --> 00:07:58,006
what happens when we do.

115
00:07:58,456 --> 00:08:02,104
We'll start with class methods, also
called static methods.

116
00:08:03,254 --> 00:08:08,041
A class method belongs to the class
itself, not to any one object. In its

117
00:08:08,041 --> 00:08:10,337
header, you'll see the word static.

118
00:08:10,787 --> 00:08:16,321
To call it from another class, write the
class name, a dot, then the method name

119
00:08:16,321 --> 00:08:20,472
and arguments. For example, Math dot
square root of sixteen.

120
00:08:20,922 --> 00:08:24,357
Inside the same class, you can just use
the method's name.

121
00:08:25,507 --> 00:08:30,989
Here's a class method called square. It
takes an int x and returns x times x.

122
00:08:31,439 --> 00:08:37,071
When main calls square of four, execution
jumps into the method. x is set to four.

123
00:08:37,521 --> 00:08:42,942
The method computes sixteen and returns
it. Execution jumps back to main, right

124
00:08:42,942 --> 00:08:44,177
where it left off.

125
00:08:45,327 --> 00:08:48,506
A returned value can be used anywhere a
value fits.

126
00:08:48,956 --> 00:08:54,350
Here, y equals square of four, plus one.
The call becomes sixteen, so y is

127
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:55,079
seventeen.

128
00:08:55,529 --> 00:09:00,886
If you call a non void method but ignore
its result, the value is simply thrown

129
00:09:00,886 --> 00:09:01,225
away.

130
00:09:02,375 --> 00:09:07,695
A void method is called as its own
statement. It does its job, then control

131
00:09:07,695 --> 00:09:08,263
returns.

132
00:09:08,713 --> 00:09:13,590
Here, print line calls happen in order:
first Start, then the method's output,

133
00:09:13,590 --> 00:09:14,153
then End.

134
00:09:15,303 --> 00:09:20,337
Method calls can even be nested. Java
evaluates the inner call first.

135
00:09:20,787 --> 00:09:25,737
In square of square of two, the inside
gives four, and the outside gives sixteen.

136
00:09:26,887 --> 00:09:33,048
Your turn. Twice takes n and returns two
times n. What is r after r equals twice of

137
00:09:33,048 --> 00:09:34,161
twice of three?

138
00:09:34,611 --> 00:09:35,891
Pause the video now.

139
00:09:36,341 --> 00:09:40,928
The inner call gives six. The outer call
gives twelve. So r is twelve.

140
00:09:41,378 --> 00:09:43,469
Next time, we'll use the Math class.

141
00:09:44,619 --> 00:09:49,295
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

142
00:09:49,295 --> 00:09:50,571
miss the next lesson.

143
00:09:51,021 --> 00:09:51,917
See you there.

